// ethiopic-demo — Ethiopic (Amharic, Tigrinya, Ge'ez) typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/ethiopic-demo.script
//
// Ethiopic is the one non-Latin script texish sets that needs no shaping at all.
// It is written left to right, so there is no bidirectional reordering; it is not
// cursive, so there are no joined forms; and — unlike every Indic script here —
// it has no dependent vowel signs to reorder or position, because the vowel is
// written into the letterform itself.
//
// That is what a syllabary means. Where Devanagari writes a consonant and hangs a
// matra on it, Ethiopic has a separate letter for each consonant-and-vowel pair:
// the base consonant and its six vowel orders are seven distinct glyphs, drawn as
// variations on one shape. The cost is a large character set — some 350 glyphs in
// daily use, spilling out of the main block into three more — and the saving is
// that one codepoint is one glyph and nothing has to be rearranged. Ethiopic text
// takes the plain path through the engine and breaks at its spaces, exactly as a
// roman paragraph does.
//
// \font ethiopic selects the bundled Noto Serif Ethiopic face, in a regular and a
// bold cut; \font amharic and \font tigrinya are aliases for the same family.

\use{document}

\title{The Ethiopic Script in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting an Ethiopic paragraph}

Each character is one glyph and the glyphs are set left to right, so a paragraph needs
nothing from the engine beyond the ordinary line breaker --- the breaks fall at the
interword spaces just as they do for Latin text.

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 14 regular
አማርኛ በኢትዮጵያ የሚነገር ቋንቋ ነው። ከግራ ወደ ቀኝ ይጻፋል። እያንዳንዱ ፊደል አንድ ተነባቢና አንድ አናባቢ
አንድ ላይ ይዞ ይጻፋል። ስለዚህ የፊደሉ ቅርጽ ራሱ አናባቢውን ያሳያል።
}

\section{The seven orders}

A letter's vowel is written into its shape. Each row below is one consonant through its
seven orders --- \italic{hä}, \italic{hu}, \italic{hi}, \italic{ha}, \italic{he},
\italic{hə}, \italic{ho} --- and the family resemblance down each row is the whole design
of the script:

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
ሀ ሁ ሂ ሃ ሄ ህ ሆ
}

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
ለ ሉ ሊ ላ ሌ ል ሎ
}

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
መ ሙ ሚ ማ ሜ ም ሞ
}

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
ሰ ሱ ሲ ሳ ሴ ስ ሶ
}

\section{Punctuation and numerals}

Ethiopic has its own marks and its own numerals. The wordspace, the full stop, the comma
and the semicolon are typed literally --- no ASCII shorthand produces them:

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
፡ ። ፣ ፤ ፥ ፦
}

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
፩ ፪ ፫ ፬ ፭ ፮ ፯ ፰ ፱ ፲
}

\section{The languages the script carries}

Amharic is the largest, but the script is shared. Tigrinya draws on letters Amharic does
not use, and Ge'ez, the liturgical language, is where the writing system began:

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 16 regular
አማርኛ   ትግርኛ   ግዕዝ
}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font ethiopic 18 bold
ሰላም! ይህ በኢትዮጵያ ፊደል ተጽፏል።
}
